    {
      "priority": "P2",
      "title": "JS rules read no decrypt-only usage of the mixed libraries the map entries used to flag",
      "file": "rules/ships_encryption/js-library-encryption.yml",
      "line_start": 14,
      "line_end": 71,
      "body": "I ran a file containing `CryptoJS.AES.decrypt(...)`, `nacl.secretbox.open(...)`, `nacl.box.open(...)`, `sodium.crypto_secretbox_open_easy(...)`, `sodium.crypto_aead_xchacha20poly1305_ietf_decrypt(...)` and `openpgp.decrypt(...)`. It produced 0 matches. Before this branch, the `crypto-js`, `tweetnacl`, `libsodium-wrappers` and `openpgp` map entries flagged any app that depended on them.\nThis is inconsistent with the rest of the codebase. The same rule reads `forge.cipher.createDecipher` (line 42), and `js-platform-encryption` reads `createDecipheriv`, `privateDecrypt` and `subtle.decrypt`. The sibling Swift and Rust rules from #13318 read the open and decrypt side (`Crypto.AES.GCM.open`, `OpeningKey::new`, `decrypt_padded_mut`). The README sentence \"Its cipher and key-agreement calls are read in the code instead\" is also not met for a decrypt call.\nGo has the same gap: `openpgp.ReadMessage(...)` was not matched.",
      "id": 1,
      "disposition": "fixed",
      "rationale": "js-library-encryption now reads the open and decrypt side of every library it reads: libsodium's open_easy, box_seal_open, the three AEAD decrypts and secretstream init_pull; TweetNaCl box.open and secretbox.open; crypto-js and crypto-es decrypt; OpenPGP.js decrypt; SJCL decrypt; eccrypto decrypt; @noble/ciphers decrypt. Pinned by js-library-encryption.test.decrypt.js (14). Go's ProtonMail ReadMessage stays unread on purpose and the rule says why: it also reads a message that is only signed, to verify it, which is the false reading this branch removes (go-xcrypto-encryption makes the same call for x/crypto's OpenPGP)."
    },
