      "line_start": 11,
      "line_end": 102,
      "body": "I ran a file containing `priv.decrypt(ct, padding.OAEP(...))`, `rsa.decrypt(ct, priv)`, a PGPy `key.decrypt(msg)`, `libnacl.crypto_box_open(...)`, and a `cryptography` `dh` key's `priv.exchange(peer)`. It produced 0 matches.\nThe rule reads `rsa.encrypt` but not `rsa.decrypt`, reads `$K.encrypt(...)` under a PGPy import but not decrypt, and reads `exchange` only for `ec.ECDH()`, x25519 and x448. Only Tink's `HybridDecrypt` is read on the decrypt side. The `cryptography`, `rsa`, `libnacl` and `pgpy` map entries are gone, so code of this shape now has no evidence at all.",
      "id": 2,
      "disposition": "fixed",
      "rationale": "py-library-encryption now reads cryptography's RSA decrypt with OAEP or PKCS1v15, a finite-field dh key's exchange under a dh import, python-rsa decrypt, PGPy decrypt under its import, and libnacl's box, secretbox, sealed-box and AEAD opens. Pinned by py-library-encryption.test.decrypt.py (7) and a PGPy decrypt in test.pgpy.py (3)."
    },
    {
      "priority": "P2",
      "title": "node-forge RSA encryption is read only when the scheme is a string literal, so the documented default call is missed",
      "file": "rules/ships_encryption/js-library-encryption.yml",
      "line_start": 43,
      "line_end": 46,
      "body": "In node-forge, `publicKey.encrypt(bytes)` defaults to `RSAES-PKCS1-V1_5`. My probe file had four calls:\n- `publicKey.encrypt(bytes)` did not match.\n- `publicKey.encrypt(bytes, scheme)` with a variable scheme did not match.\n- `forge.pki.encryptRsaPrivateKey(...)` did not match.\n- `publicKey.encrypt(bytes, 'RSAES-PKCS1-V1_5')` matched; it was the only match.\nThis branch also removed the `forge.rsa.generateKeyPair` and `forge.pki.rsa.generateKeyPair` patterns and the `node-forge` map entry. An app that generates a key pair and calls `publicKey.encrypt(bytes)` now yields nothing.",
      "id": 3,
      "disposition": "fixed",
      "rationale": "Any .encrypt or .decrypt call in a file that imports node-forge (ES import, namespace import or require) is now read, which covers publicKey.encrypt(bytes) with the default RSAES-PKCS1-V1_5 and a scheme held in a variable; forge.pki.rsa.decrypt and the literal-scheme decrypts were added too. Pinned by js-library-encryption.test.forge.js (4). encryptRsaPrivateKey is left unread: it protects a private key with a password, which is access control under the activity's definition."
    },
    {
      "priority": "P2",
      "title": "phpseclib RSA encryption is read only in one narrow spelling, with no alias branch",
      "file": "rules/ships_encryption/php-library-encryption.yml",
      "line_start": 146,
      "line_end": 171,
      "body": "The new branches match only `->withPadding(RSA::ENCRYPTION_OAEP)` or `ENCRYPTION_PKCS1` passed alone, and `DH::computeSecret` under a non-aliased `use`. Probes with 0 matches:\n- `PublicKeyLoader::load($pem)->encrypt($m)` with no `withPadding`. As I recall phpseclib 3, its default is `ENCRYPTION_OAEP`.\n- `RSA::createKey()->getPublicKey()->encrypt($m)`.\n- `->withPadding(RSA::ENCRYPTION_OAEP | RSA::SIGNATURE_PSS)`, which combines two padding flags.\n- `use phpseclib3\\Crypt\\RSA as Rsa;` followed by `Rsa::ENCRYPTION_OAEP`, and `use phpseclib3\\Crypt\\DH as KeyAgreement;` followed by `KeyAgreement::computeSecret(...)`.\n- v2 `new \\phpseclib\\Crypt\\RSA(); ...->encrypt(...)`.\nThe file's header says each class is read three ways (qualified, short name, alias). These two branches have no alias-positive branch, unlike every other branch in the file. The `composer.yml` comment says the rule reads \"RSA encryption ... in use\", but it covers only a small slice of how RSA encryption is written. The `phpseclib/phpseclib` map entry that caught all of it is removed.",
      "id": 4,
      "disposition": "fixed",
      "rationale": "phpseclib RSA: a key's encrypt or decrypt in a file that imports phpseclib3 or phpseclib RSA or PublicKeyLoader (aliased or not), on a fully qualified loader or RSA call (metavariable-pattern on the receiver), and on a variable holding new \\phpseclib\\Crypt\\RSA() or a qualified loader; withPadding now reads ENCRYPTION_OAEP or ENCRYPTION_PKCS1 anywhere in its argument, so combined flags and aliases match. DH::computeSecret gained an alias branch. The engine treats -> and :: alike in patterns, so the receiver must hold $ or ( and Laravel's Crypt::encrypt in the existing negative fixture stays unmatched. Pinned by test.phpseclib-rsa.php (4) and test.phpseclib-fq.php (3)."
    },
